00:08:11.461 --> 00:08:13.900
So let's talk a little bit about
neural nets and deep learning,

00:08:13.901 --> 00:08:17.450
because as I said,
they have been so revolutionary.

00:08:17.451 --> 00:08:20.210
The interesting thing is
that as probably many of

00:08:20.211 --> 00:08:23.000
you know, it's not the first
time that neural nets have

00:08:23.001 --> 00:08:24.310
captured people's attention.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:31.030
Neural nets were actually,
back in the 1980s, when

00:08:31.031 --> 00:08:34.870
I was a teenager, that's what
really got me excited about AI.

00:08:34.871 --> 00:08:38.060
Neural nets were going through
a big wave of excitement

00:08:38.061 --> 00:08:40.550
in the middle to late 80s.

00:08:40.551 --> 00:08:43.000
When I was an undergraduate and

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.850
that wave of excitement kinda
died down and is come back up.

00:08:47.851 --> 00:08:49.900
So what are neural nets?

00:08:49.901 --> 00:08:51.728
Just to clarify, what we mean,

00:08:51.729 --> 00:08:54.386
they're many different
kinds of neural net.

00:08:54.387 --> 00:08:58.934
But the main kind of neural
network that people work with

00:08:58.935 --> 00:09:03.578
right now in the deep learning
community are essentially

00:09:03.579 --> 00:09:08.140
tunable, nonlinear function
approximators, okay?

00:09:08.141 --> 00:09:12.339
So a neural net is tunable,
nonlinear functions with many

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:16.463
parameters, but we could
call these parameters theta.

00:09:16.464 --> 00:09:18.121
I like to use
the symbol theta for

00:09:18.122 --> 00:09:21.610
parameters cuz that's often
what's done in statistics.

00:09:21.611 --> 00:09:25.650
In a neural net, that would be
the weights in your neural net.

00:09:25.651 --> 00:09:30.860
And generally, neural nets model
some input-output relationship.

00:09:30.861 --> 00:09:33.160
That's what I mean by
the nonlinear function.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:38.093
In this case, maybe the inputs
are x and the outputs are y.

00:09:38.094 --> 00:09:42.952
And interestingly, neural nets
tend to be nonlinear, both

00:09:42.953 --> 00:09:47.826
in terms of the parameters theta
and in terms of the inputs x.

00:09:47.827 --> 00:09:51.210
The simplest kind of neural
net is a very old idea.

00:09:51.211 --> 00:09:54.696
This is the equation for
logistic regression

00:09:54.697 --> 00:09:59.006
That's basically a neural
net that takes some inputs,

00:09:59.007 --> 00:10:02.858
does some linear projection
of those inputs, and

00:10:02.859 --> 00:10:06.711
then maps that into
a nonlinearity that turns into

00:10:06.712 --> 00:10:10.029
a probability of a class
being one or zero.

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:14.609
For example, if you're doing
classification between, I don't

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:18.630
know, images of cats versus
dogs or something like that.

00:10:18.631 --> 00:10:23.150
So that's the simplest
neural net I could think of.

00:10:23.151 --> 00:10:25.575
And if you think of
most neural networks,

00:10:25.576 --> 00:10:29.040
essentially, what they are,
are concatenations of these

00:10:29.041 --> 00:10:31.548
sorts of simple,
primitive operations.

00:10:31.549 --> 00:10:36.180
So a multi-layer neural net,
this one only has one layer.

00:10:36.181 --> 00:10:40.356
A multi-layer neural net
models the overall function,

00:10:40.357 --> 00:10:43.401
from x to y as
a composition of functions,

00:10:43.402 --> 00:10:46.622
each of those are the layers
in the network.

00:10:46.623 --> 00:10:49.491
And here is an example of
a two layer version of that,

00:10:49.492 --> 00:10:53.570
which might correspond to the
network that looks like this.

00:10:53.571 --> 00:10:56.584
The first layer might have
elements that look like this.

00:10:56.585 --> 00:10:59.986
And then you might linerally
combine them in a second layer

00:10:59.987 --> 00:11:02.460
to produce the outputs, okay?

00:11:02.461 --> 00:11:04.905
In this case, maybe with some
Gaussian noise, in this case,

00:11:04.906 --> 00:11:05.810
for classification.

00:11:05.811 --> 00:11:10.169
The details don't matter,
you can mix and

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:15.180
match these various
forms of neural net.

00:11:15.181 --> 00:11:17.194
So these are nonlinear
tunable functions,

00:11:17.195 --> 00:11:18.560
well what do you do with them?

00:11:18.561 --> 00:11:20.350
How do you train them?

00:11:20.351 --> 00:11:23.917
Generally, almost all
training algorithms for

00:11:23.918 --> 00:11:28.100
neural networks can be seen
as maximizing a likelihood.

00:11:28.101 --> 00:11:32.921
The likelihood is the
probability of the outputs given

00:11:32.922 --> 00:11:37.846
the inputs, or maybe a penalized
likelihood if you're

00:11:37.847 --> 00:11:40.804
trying to avoid over-fitting.

00:11:40.805 --> 00:11:44.650
Using some variation on
stochastic gradient descent

00:11:44.651 --> 00:11:45.850
optimization.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:51.557
Maximizing likelihoods is an
idea that's about 100 years old

00:11:51.558 --> 00:11:52.746
in statistics.

00:11:52.747 --> 00:11:55.585
Gradient descent methods
are probably one of

00:11:55.586 --> 00:11:58.526
the simplest forms
of optimization.

00:11:58.527 --> 00:12:01.192
And so
boiled down to it's essence,

00:12:01.193 --> 00:12:05.753
a neutral network is simply a
nonlinear function approximated

00:12:05.754 --> 00:12:09.711
combined with a little bit
of very basic statistics and

00:12:09.712 --> 00:12:12.737
a little bit of very
basic optimization.

00:12:12.738 --> 00:12:15.325
The interesting thing is that
a lot of statisticians are very

00:12:15.326 --> 00:12:16.967
confused about what
neural nets are.

00:12:16.968 --> 00:12:18.900
I've had statisticians
come up to me and say, so

00:12:18.901 --> 00:12:20.800
what's deep learning,
what's neural nets?

00:12:20.801 --> 00:12:25.592
And then I explain this to them,
and they say, is that it?

00:12:25.593 --> 00:12:27.803
And then they're like, well,
then I'm not interested, and

00:12:27.804 --> 00:12:28.563
they sort of move on.

00:12:28.564 --> 00:12:31.769
Whereas, we kind of know in
machine learning that even

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.975
though these are incredibly
simple, boring things,

00:12:34.976 --> 00:12:36.926
if you throw enough
data at them,

00:12:36.927 --> 00:12:39.736
they'll do really
interesting stuff, okay?

00:12:39.737 --> 00:12:43.610
So that's what they are,
and what's been happening?

00:12:43.611 --> 00:12:48.690
What is different between,
say the late 80s

00:12:48.691 --> 00:12:53.249
when I was playing on
using neural nets for

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:57.170
parsing natural language and
now?

00:12:57.171 --> 00:13:00.060
Well, there are a whole bunch
of things that are different.

00:13:00.061 --> 00:13:03.599
So one of them is the major
rebranding exercise, and I

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:07.860
applaud the people who manage to
pull this off against all odds.

00:13:07.861 --> 00:13:10.570
So now we call it deep learning.

00:13:10.571 --> 00:13:13.721
Cuz people though neural
networks were kind of old and

00:13:13.722 --> 00:13:17.515
passe, but deep learning sounded
like something new and deep.

00:13:17.516 --> 00:13:22.460
And so the rebranding
exercise worked really well.

00:13:22.461 --> 00:13:25.147
But there are a whole bunch of
new ideas as well that were

00:13:25.148 --> 00:13:25.800
developed.

00:13:25.801 --> 00:13:30.573
Some innovations in terms of
architectures, in particular,

00:13:30.574 --> 00:13:33.760
going deeper, using more layers.

00:13:33.761 --> 00:13:38.146
Using different activation
functions instead of sigmoid,

00:13:38.147 --> 00:13:39.700
rectified linears.

00:13:39.701 --> 00:13:43.830
New ways of regularizing them
with things like drop out.

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:48.107
Rediscoveries of some old and

00:13:48.108 --> 00:13:53.190
kind of interesting ideas like
LSTMs for time series, etc.

00:13:53.191 --> 00:13:55.186
So there were some innovations,

00:13:55.187 --> 00:13:57.904
and the rate of innovation
just keeps going.

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:00.857
But importantly,

00:14:00.858 --> 00:14:05.871
there are just vastly
larger data sets right now.

00:14:05.872 --> 00:14:09.440
Really web-scale data that you
can throw at these things.

00:14:09.441 --> 00:14:12.760
And along with that, we have
vastly larger compute resources.

00:14:12.761 --> 00:14:18.350
We can actually run stuff
on the cloud, with GPUs.

00:14:18.351 --> 00:14:22.384
People now talk about like
hundreds of cores on a single

00:14:22.385 --> 00:14:24.928
experiment and things like that,

00:14:24.929 --> 00:14:28.538
that were unimaginable
a couple of decades ago.

00:14:28.539 --> 00:14:31.855
We also have much
better software tools,

00:14:31.856 --> 00:14:36.501
things that have really managed
to democratize people's

00:14:36.502 --> 00:14:41.350
ability to run neural networks
and reproduce experiments.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:46.203
To the extent that you could be
a high school student with good

00:14:46.204 --> 00:14:47.593
programming skills and

00:14:47.594 --> 00:14:50.522
not really even understand
what a derivative is.

00:14:50.523 --> 00:14:55.507
And you can reproduce somebody's
blog post on neural nets, and

00:14:55.508 --> 00:14:59.040
think, wow, cool, I'm doing AI. Right?

00:14:59.041 --> 00:15:01.582
And that's really what's

00:15:01.583 --> 00:15:06.930
transformed the inflow of
excitement and interest.

00:15:06.931 --> 00:15:10.955
That's also translated into
vastly increased industry

00:15:10.956 --> 00:15:14.978
investment, just because
these systems have started to

00:15:14.979 --> 00:15:17.009
produce exciting results.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:21.553
And along with that,
there's been massive media hype.

00:15:21.554 --> 00:15:25.185
Okay, and the media hype,
the many ways of explaining it.

00:15:25.186 --> 00:15:28.885
One of them is when companies
start spending hundreds of

00:15:28.886 --> 00:15:31.246
millions of dollars
on something,

00:15:31.247 --> 00:15:33.440
the media pays attention.

00:15:33.441 --> 00:15:36.340
If it looks like it has
something to do with

00:15:36.341 --> 00:15:39.260
the brain and Terminator and
stuff like that,

00:15:39.261 --> 00:15:41.690
then the media go totally crazy.

00:15:41.691 --> 00:15:44.500
And you can imagine all the
stories that come out of that.

00:15:46.635 --> 00:15:49.646
Of course it feeds on itself,
and then the next company

00:15:49.647 --> 00:15:52.470
comes along and says,
no, we're missing out.

00:15:52.471 --> 00:15:56.859
We need to spend a few hundred
million on this as well, right?

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:59.787
Okay so, this is what's happened
since the 80s and 90s, and

00:15:59.788 --> 00:16:01.290
it really has been a revolution.

00:16:01.291 --> 00:16:06.356
I wanna be grounded in reality,
but I don't wanna minimize

00:16:06.357 --> 00:16:10.827
from the fact that it has
had a major, major impact.

00:16:10.828 --> 00:16:15.332
But it's important not to
get caught up in the fantasy

00:16:15.333 --> 00:16:19.490
that AI is solved, or
something like that.

00:16:19.491 --> 00:16:22.251
If you actually look at
these nonlinear function

00:16:22.252 --> 00:16:25.091
approximators, they have
a lot of limitations.

00:16:25.092 --> 00:16:26.771
And here are a few of
their limitations.

00:16:26.772 --> 00:16:31.419
So, one of them is that neural
nets tend to be, by and

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:34.280
large, very very data hungry.

00:16:34.281 --> 00:16:36.929
Humans are pretty good at
learning from a few examples.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:41.745
And neural nets, in most cases,
need large numbers of examples,

00:16:41.746 --> 00:16:44.860
sometimes millions of examples.

00:16:44.861 --> 00:16:47.460
They're are also extremely
compute-intensive.

00:16:47.461 --> 00:16:48.762
So if you think about, again,

00:16:48.763 --> 00:16:53.800
to use our poor primate brains
as an example, we operate

00:16:53.801 --> 00:16:57.740
with the power consumption
of a light bulb basically.

00:16:57.741 --> 00:17:03.240
And if you look at
a cloud full of GPUs or

00:17:03.241 --> 00:17:06.210
something, that's using
massive amounts of energy and

00:17:06.211 --> 00:17:09.280
compute to solve fairly
simple problems.

00:17:09.281 --> 00:17:11.922
Neural nets and deep learning
systems are actually quite poor

00:17:11.923 --> 00:17:13.305
at representing uncertainty.

00:17:13.306 --> 00:17:16.416
And this is something that
really bothers me, and

00:17:16.417 --> 00:17:19.115
I'll talk about in
some detail very soon.

00:17:19.116 --> 00:17:23.415
They're also easily fooled
by adversarial examples.

00:17:23.416 --> 00:17:25.561
So, how many people have seen,
here,

00:17:25.562 --> 00:17:27.730
adversarial examples
in neural nets.

00:17:27.731 --> 00:17:29.470
Raise your hand if
you've seen them.

00:17:29.471 --> 00:17:30.595
Raise your hand if you
haven't seen them.

00:17:30.596 --> 00:17:32.027
Okay.

00:17:35.771 --> 00:17:36.616
I need to account for

00:17:36.617 --> 00:17:39.800
the fact that some people just
don't like raising their hands.

00:17:39.801 --> 00:17:41.480
You always have to
ask both questions.

00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:48.449
So as you've seen, adversarial
examples are examples that

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:52.175
are either perceptually
indistinguishable

00:17:52.176 --> 00:17:56.781
from another example, but
that give the neural network

00:17:56.782 --> 00:18:01.681
a wildly different output, or
that the examples are wildly

00:18:01.682 --> 00:18:05.424
different from anything
that you've seen.

00:18:05.425 --> 00:18:08.703
And the neural network is
confidentially giving you some

00:18:08.704 --> 00:18:10.690
output, some response for that.

00:18:10.691 --> 00:18:15.550
So again, it has something
to do with the neural net's

00:18:15.551 --> 00:18:17.430
inability to represent
uncertainty.

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:20.797
Neural nets are finicky
to optimize.

00:18:20.798 --> 00:18:24.820
It's actually quite hard
to do the optimization.

00:18:24.821 --> 00:18:27.630
They're generally
uninterpretable black-boxes.

00:18:27.631 --> 00:18:31.220
They lack in transparency, and
they're difficult to trust.

00:18:31.221 --> 00:18:35.033
And actually, when you look at
examples, an industry where

00:18:35.034 --> 00:18:38.923
things get deployed, if you're
just doing something fairly

00:18:38.924 --> 00:18:42.737
harmless, maybe you're just
classifying images for fun,

00:18:42.738 --> 00:18:46.423
then it's okay to be wrong and
not know why you're wrong.

00:18:46.424 --> 00:18:51.004
But if you're doing something
actually rather important,

00:18:51.005 --> 00:18:53.340
like detecting pedestrians or

00:18:53.341 --> 00:18:57.738
something like that in a
self-driving car, then the fact

00:18:57.739 --> 00:19:02.318
that you have a non-transparent
black-box system that you

00:19:02.319 --> 00:19:07.400
can't then easily debug, becomes
potentially a non-starter.

00:19:07.401 --> 00:19:11.320
You can't even think of using
that, unless you have some

00:19:11.321 --> 00:19:16.500
reasonable guarantees, and some
reasonable ways of understanding

00:19:16.501 --> 00:19:22.640
what it's doing and
debugging it.

00:19:22.641 --> 00:19:24.751
So these are some
of the limitations.

00:19:24.752 --> 00:19:29.033
And what I wanna do is, I want
to actually talk a little bit

00:19:29.034 --> 00:19:31.720
about going beyond
deep learning.

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:36.496
And of course I have my
own particular bias.

00:19:36.497 --> 00:19:38.264
There are many ways to
go beyond deep learning.

00:19:38.265 --> 00:19:40.409
And in fact, to be fair,

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:44.520
many people realize all
of these limitations.

00:19:44.521 --> 00:19:48.707
And if you talked to some of the
best research labs in the world,

00:19:48.708 --> 00:19:52.736
they're working on various
aspects of trying to solve these

00:19:52.737 --> 00:19:53.376
things.

00:19:53.377 --> 00:19:57.458
But one way of thinking about
going beyond deep learning is to

00:19:57.459 --> 00:20:00.830
take maybe a slightly
different approach.

00:20:00.831 --> 00:20:02.390
To machine learning.

00:20:02.391 --> 00:20:06.890
So, a lot of people when they
learn about machine learning,

00:20:06.891 --> 00:20:08.425
they take a course.

00:20:08.426 --> 00:20:11.270
And the course basically-
I've looked at a lot of these

00:20:11.271 --> 00:20:16.060
syllabuses around the world,
and the syllabus usually looks

00:20:16.061 --> 00:20:20.420
like- sorry,
I found the back of this.

00:20:20.421 --> 00:20:24.230
It just looks like
a toolkit of methods.

00:20:24.231 --> 00:20:26.740
So you'll learn about this,
you'll learn about random

00:20:26.741 --> 00:20:28.740
forest, then you'll
learn k-means,

00:20:28.741 --> 00:20:33.610
then you'll learn about back
propagation, then you'll learn

00:20:33.611 --> 00:20:38.460
about Q-learning, and
it's just a bag of stuff, right?

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:42.399
There a little bit of an attempt
at making some theory

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:45.700
connections, like
you probably want to

00:20:45.701 --> 00:20:50.670
have some theoretical analysis
of empirical performance,

00:20:50.671 --> 00:20:53.670
like generalization error and so
on of various different methods.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:58.470
The framework that I'm
gonna talk about which

00:20:58.471 --> 00:21:02.800
I'm quite in love with, although
it has its own limitations,

00:21:02.801 --> 00:21:06.730
is thinking about machine
learning as kind of a science

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:10.110
of learning models from data.

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:15.140
And so most things can be
put under that rubric,

00:21:15.141 --> 00:21:16.560
not everything.

00:21:16.561 --> 00:21:22.530
But the idea is that there is
sort of like an overarching

00:21:22.531 --> 00:21:25.520
scientific framework for
doing machine learning, and

00:21:25.521 --> 00:21:28.760
the details of the algorithms
are just that, they are details.

00:21:28.761 --> 00:21:32.150
They matter, but you kinda
look at the bigger picture and

00:21:32.151 --> 00:21:32.830
the bigger framework.

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:37.200
So what do I mean by
learning from data and

00:21:37.201 --> 00:21:39.220
what do I mean by modeling,
in particular?

00:21:41.110 --> 00:21:43.870
So here is this sort of
view of machine learning as

00:21:43.871 --> 00:21:45.370
probabilistic modelling.

00:21:45.371 --> 00:21:49.410
Well, let's step back and
ask ourselves what is a model?

00:21:49.411 --> 00:21:52.642
Okay, the term model is use in
many, many different fields,

00:21:52.643 --> 00:21:54.870
it means different things
to different people.

00:21:54.871 --> 00:21:58.920
&gt;&gt; But one way of
thinking about models is,

00:21:58.921 --> 00:22:01.390
a model is a description

00:22:01.391 --> 00:22:05.650
of possible data you could
observe from a system, okay?

00:22:06.890 --> 00:22:10.630
&gt;&gt; So the way you tell, it might
also be an understandable,

00:22:10.631 --> 00:22:12.140
interpretable or
something like that,

00:22:12.141 --> 00:22:15.315
like a model in the sciences or
something like that, or

00:22:15.316 --> 00:22:20.640
an economic model, or
model in biology, and so on.

00:22:20.641 --> 00:22:21.740
All of these,

00:22:21.741 --> 00:22:25.180
the term model gets used a lot,
but I think ultimately

00:22:25.181 --> 00:22:29.360
if the model can't make some
claims about potential data,

00:22:29.361 --> 00:22:31.480
then it's very hard to know
if it's a good model or not.

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:36.200
At some point, you have to
ground the model in data, and

00:22:36.201 --> 00:22:40.600
not the data that you had before
because very easy to say,

00:22:40.601 --> 00:22:42.940
my model fits my
old data perfectly.

00:22:42.941 --> 00:22:44.920
The model should say
something about data you

00:22:44.921 --> 00:22:45.850
haven't observed yet.

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:52.710
Now because of that,

00:22:52.711 --> 00:22:58.160
modeling is intimately tied to
the concept of uncertainty.

00:22:58.161 --> 00:23:02.256
So to make any claims about
data we haven't observed yet,

00:23:02.257 --> 00:23:06.520
we have to be honest about our
uncertainty in terms of the data

00:23:06.521 --> 00:23:08.690
we haven't observed yet.

00:23:08.691 --> 00:23:12.880
You can't have, for example,
a weather forecasting model that

00:23:12.881 --> 00:23:15.950
gives you a precise temperature
for tomorrow without tell you

00:23:15.951 --> 00:23:19.110
what it's uncertainty is,
it just doesn't make much sense.

00:23:19.111 --> 00:23:21.910
So we're gonna use
the mathematics of probability

00:23:21.911 --> 00:23:25.120
theory to express all
forms of uncertainty and

00:23:25.121 --> 00:23:26.980
noise associated with our model.

00:23:26.981 --> 00:23:30.700
And then, it turns out that,
that same mathematical framework

00:23:30.701 --> 00:23:36.020
can be used to do inferences
about unknown quantities, adapt

00:23:36.021 --> 00:23:38.880
our models, make predictions,
and learn from data.

00:23:38.881 --> 00:23:42.000
And all of this used to
be called probability.

00:23:42.001 --> 00:23:44.220
This part used to maybe
sometimes we called inverse

00:23:44.221 --> 00:23:48.490
probability until early
in the 20th century when

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:53.580
Fisher, who's a classical,

00:23:53.581 --> 00:23:57.190
sort of one of the founders
of classical statistics said,

00:23:57.191 --> 00:23:59.750
that probability,
that inverse probability,

00:23:59.751 --> 00:24:02.455
you're just followers of
Thomas Bayes, you're Bayesians.

00:24:02.456 --> 00:24:05.980
And he coined, actually he
coined the term Bayesian as

00:24:05.981 --> 00:24:10.680
a derogatory term to distinguish
himself because he was

00:24:10.681 --> 00:24:13.310
creating a new view
on statistics.

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:19.030
So we can call it inverse
probability if we wanna go back

00:24:19.031 --> 00:24:24.640
in time, and the framework
basically boils down to a very,

00:24:24.641 --> 00:24:26.090
very simple set of concepts.

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:33.450
So in the world of probabilistic
modeling and Bayesian machine

00:24:33.451 --> 00:24:37.700
learning and so on, there
are only two kinds of things.

00:24:37.701 --> 00:24:42.420
There are stuff you observe or
measure,

00:24:42.421 --> 00:24:45.530
that's what you call data, and
then there's everything else.

00:24:45.531 --> 00:24:48.760
Everything else is stuff
that you didn't measure.

00:24:48.761 --> 00:24:50.006
So necessarily,

00:24:50.007 --> 00:24:54.333
you have to be uncertain about
the stuff you didn't measure.

00:24:54.334 --> 00:24:58.389
And we're going to use
the mathematics of probability

00:24:58.390 --> 00:25:01.926
theory to represent all
forms of uncertainty.

00:25:01.927 --> 00:25:05.672
Just like we use calculus to
represent rates of change,

00:25:05.673 --> 00:25:09.509
probability theory is
the mathematics of uncertainly.

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:13.404
And actually don't like
the term randomness

00:25:13.405 --> 00:25:16.794
because it connotes
something weird.

00:25:16.795 --> 00:25:18.890
Actually, I don't
believe much is random.

00:25:18.891 --> 00:25:22.030
For example, I can be uncertain
about how many people are in

00:25:22.031 --> 00:25:23.320
this room, right?

00:25:23.321 --> 00:25:26.579
I'm certainly uncertain
about that, but

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:30.741
the number of people in this
room is not random in a usual

00:25:30.742 --> 00:25:33.106
colloquial sense of the word.

00:25:33.107 --> 00:25:34.946
And when I look at this room,

00:25:34.947 --> 00:25:39.110
that's data that my brain gets
about the number of people here.

00:25:39.111 --> 00:25:41.792
My brain is trying
to process that.

00:25:41.793 --> 00:25:46.680
And I'm guessing maybe there
are between, I don't know,

00:25:46.681 --> 00:25:49.530
40 and 90 people in this room.

00:25:49.531 --> 00:25:52.694
Probably more than 40, I don't
know, maybe between 50 and 90.

00:25:52.695 --> 00:25:54.990
All right, so how do I
represent that uncertainty?

00:25:54.991 --> 00:26:00.780
Well, the idea in
probabilistic modeling is use

00:26:00.781 --> 00:26:05.100
probability distributions for
all forms of uncertainty, okay?

00:26:05.101 --> 00:26:07.430
So you use probability
distributions for uncertainty.

00:26:07.431 --> 00:26:09.610
You get data, for
example, I'm looking.

00:26:09.611 --> 00:26:13.380
And I might collect more data,
let's say I start counting.

00:26:13.381 --> 00:26:16.310
That's just
collecting more data.

00:26:16.311 --> 00:26:20.090
And at some point,
my uncertainty collapses.

00:26:20.091 --> 00:26:26.020
Learning is the process
of going from a state

00:26:26.021 --> 00:26:30.560
of knowledge before observing
the data, that's the prior,

00:26:30.561 --> 00:26:33.160
to a state of knowledge
after observing the data.

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:37.310
And the process by which you go
through that is a very simple

00:26:37.311 --> 00:26:37.870
process.

00:26:37.871 --> 00:26:40.577
You take your prior, which is
the distribution over your

00:26:40.578 --> 00:26:43.050
uncertain quantities,
your hypotheses.

00:26:43.051 --> 00:26:45.080
You multiply it by
the likelihood,

00:26:45.081 --> 00:26:49.258
which is a probability
of the observed data for

00:26:49.259 --> 00:26:51.350
any one of your hypotheses.

00:26:51.351 --> 00:26:53.790
Like the number of people
in the room would be

00:26:53.791 --> 00:26:56.330
various hypotheses
I could entertain.

00:26:56.331 --> 00:26:58.190
You multiply the prior
by the likelihood.

00:26:58.191 --> 00:27:00.950
You renormalized summing over
all the hypotheses you're

00:27:00.951 --> 00:27:02.480
willing to consider.

00:27:02.481 --> 00:27:05.276
And then you get the posterior
distribution over hypotheses

00:27:05.277 --> 00:27:05.886
given data.

00:27:05.887 --> 00:27:09.320
It's as straightforward as that.

00:27:09.321 --> 00:27:13.650
And the beauty of this is
that we can use this as

00:27:13.651 --> 00:27:19.080
a unifying principle for
a lot of machine learning.

00:27:19.081 --> 00:27:22.044
Not necessarily for the parts
of machine learning that involve

00:27:22.045 --> 00:27:25.050
decision-making, like
reinforcement learning.

00:27:25.051 --> 00:27:29.010
That involves an additional
thing that we need to add on top

00:27:29.011 --> 00:27:31.560
of this Bayesian framework.

00:27:31.561 --> 00:27:35.450
But for most of the rest
of machine learning,

00:27:35.451 --> 00:27:38.520
we can actually use this to
understand what's going on.

00:27:38.521 --> 00:27:41.260
So it turns out actually
Bayes' rule isn't even

00:27:41.261 --> 00:27:42.290
a fundamental rule.

00:27:42.291 --> 00:27:45.970
Bayes' rule is a corollary of
two even-simpler rules from

00:27:45.971 --> 00:27:49.190
probability theory, the sum
rule and the product rule.

00:27:49.191 --> 00:27:50.240
And here are the sum rule and

00:27:50.241 --> 00:27:52.490
the product rule written
out in a boring form,

00:27:52.491 --> 00:27:54.570
as you might find in a textbook.

00:27:54.571 --> 00:27:58.307
Sum rule says that the
probability of some variable is

00:27:58.308 --> 00:28:02.211
the sum or integral if that
other variable is continuous

00:28:02.212 --> 00:28:05.960
over some other variable y
of the joint probability.

00:28:05.961 --> 00:28:09.932
And the product rule says that
the joint probability of two

00:28:09.933 --> 00:28:13.742
variables, x and y, can be
factored into this marginal

00:28:13.743 --> 00:28:17.312
probability of x times
the conditional probability

00:28:17.313 --> 00:28:19.926
of y given x, or
the other way around.

00:28:19.927 --> 00:28:23.710
The basic rules you might
learn about in a textbook.

00:28:23.711 --> 00:28:27.721
If we take these x's and y's,
and we replace them by things

00:28:27.722 --> 00:28:30.942
that we might actually
might be interested in.

00:28:30.943 --> 00:28:37.042
Like D for data, theta for
parameters of a model.

00:28:37.043 --> 00:28:41.464
And then condition everything on
m, which may be like the model

00:28:41.465 --> 00:28:44.810
class like what we
are currently considering.

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:50.280
Then we get a single equation,
this is now Bayes' rule,

00:28:50.281 --> 00:28:53.110
that defines learning
from data for parameters.

00:28:53.111 --> 00:28:56.280
And it says, well, you have
a model with some parameters.

00:28:56.281 --> 00:28:58.870
Before observing the data,
you have a prior.

00:28:58.871 --> 00:29:00.840
Which represents your
uncertainty about

00:29:00.841 --> 00:29:02.840
what those parameters could be.

00:29:02.841 --> 00:29:04.891
Then you observe the data, so

00:29:04.892 --> 00:29:08.264
you multiply the prior
by this likelihood term.

00:29:08.265 --> 00:29:11.568
And then you renormalize it with
this interesting quantity called

00:29:11.569 --> 00:29:13.680
the marginal likelihood.

00:29:13.681 --> 00:29:17.380
And that gives you the posterior
over parameters given the data.

00:29:17.381 --> 00:29:19.045
It's very straightforward.

00:29:19.046 --> 00:29:23.317
If you had to make predictions,
then probability theory tells

00:29:23.318 --> 00:29:27.426
you that to predict any unknown
quantity, call that unknown

00:29:27.427 --> 00:29:31.470
quantity x, given the data
that you've observed.

00:29:31.471 --> 00:29:34.110
Then the sum and product rule
tell you there's only one and

00:29:34.111 --> 00:29:36.250
only one way of
doing prediction.

00:29:36.251 --> 00:29:40.101
And that is average or
integrate the predictions for

00:29:40.102 --> 00:29:44.216
different parameter values
weighted by this posterior

00:29:44.217 --> 00:29:48.857
probability over parameter
values that you've considered in

00:29:48.858 --> 00:29:50.451
the equation above.

00:29:50.452 --> 00:29:53.669
So prediction in the Bayesian
framework is naturally

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:56.814
an ensembling process
where you're averaging, or

00:29:56.815 --> 00:29:59.754
voting together,
many different predictors.

00:29:59.755 --> 00:30:04.371
And model comparison is
essentially just the application

00:30:04.372 --> 00:30:07.667
of Bayes rule at a level
suddenly higher up

00:30:07.668 --> 00:30:10.696
from parameters to
a level of models.

00:30:10.697 --> 00:30:15.451
Like, for example, are there,
I don't know, three or four

00:30:15.452 --> 00:30:20.310
clusters in the data or should
I have 10 or 20 hidden units?

00:30:20.311 --> 00:30:22.935
These are all modeling
questions, right, and

00:30:22.936 --> 00:30:26.160
you could answer them in
principle using this framework.

00:30:26.161 --> 00:30:27.662
So any questions about that?

00:30:27.663 --> 00:30:33.707
[BLANK AUDIO]
&gt;&gt; Can you be more specific with

00:30:33.708 --> 00:30:37.629
why the enforcement learning
doesn't fit into this framework?

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:42.551
&gt;&gt; So
the framework I've talked about

00:30:42.552 --> 00:30:47.315
talks about inference from data.

00:30:47.316 --> 00:30:51.915
Think about that as
knowledge acquisition or

00:30:51.916 --> 00:30:54.100
learning from data.

00:30:54.101 --> 00:30:57.490
Reinforcement learning
falls into decision theory.

00:30:57.491 --> 00:30:59.162
So actually,
it's not incompatible,

00:30:59.163 --> 00:31:00.400
it's just that it's a part.

00:31:00.401 --> 00:31:02.206
So there's Bayesian
inference and

00:31:02.207 --> 00:31:04.640
there's Bayesian
decision theory.

00:31:04.641 --> 00:31:08.274
Bayesian decision theory says
one side inferred everything I'm

00:31:08.275 --> 00:31:09.204
interested in.

00:31:09.205 --> 00:31:14.296
Now maybe there is a loss
function which tells me what

00:31:14.297 --> 00:31:19.631
the loss is for various
different states of the world.

00:31:19.632 --> 00:31:24.088
And I should take actions that
minimize my expected loss, and

00:31:24.089 --> 00:31:26.670
that's reinforcement learning.

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:29.540
Loss, reward,
it's the same thing.

00:31:29.541 --> 00:31:31.083
It's not immediate reward,

00:31:31.084 --> 00:31:33.208
you're looking at
long-term reward.

00:31:33.209 --> 00:31:37.013
So it's Bayesian sequential
decision theory would be how we

00:31:37.014 --> 00:31:40.519
would think about any sequential
decision problem as in

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:43.738
reinforcement learning,
and it's nothing new.

00:31:43.739 --> 00:31:45.870
Been around for
60 years at least, yeah.

00:31:45.871 --> 00:31:49.209
&gt;&gt; I've got a question about
the learning behaviors.

00:31:49.210 --> 00:31:53.227
So what we're trying to
learn is the posterior of

00:31:53.228 --> 00:31:57.111
the parameters given
some observed data?

00:31:57.112 --> 00:31:59.404
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Is it assumed that the other

00:31:59.405 --> 00:32:04.183
quantities in that equation
are all given beforehand, so

00:32:04.184 --> 00:32:07.109
the likelihood of
the parameters in

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:10.730
the model m is something
that we're given?

00:32:10.731 --> 00:32:13.634
&gt;&gt; So usually, this term here,

00:32:13.635 --> 00:32:18.869
the likelihood is something
that we assume is known.

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:21.004
But the funny thing
is that actually,

00:32:21.005 --> 00:32:24.477
we shouldn't really necessarily
assume that the likelihood

00:32:24.478 --> 00:32:25.890
function is even known.

00:32:25.891 --> 00:32:28.687
Maybe the likelihood function
has some parameters, but

00:32:28.688 --> 00:32:29.625
you have to infer.

00:32:29.626 --> 00:32:32.827
But if you consider a family
of likelihood functions,

00:32:32.828 --> 00:32:36.376
you can just say that they're
just extra parameters you have

00:32:36.377 --> 00:32:39.925
to infer from data that allow
you to choose between different

00:32:39.926 --> 00:32:41.450
likelihood functions.

00:32:41.451 --> 00:32:43.080
It gets a little bit subtle.

00:32:43.081 --> 00:32:48.320
But just like in, basically,
in almost all of statistics and

00:32:48.321 --> 00:32:53.650
also machine learning, people
assume that the likelihood or

00:32:53.651 --> 00:32:56.080
the loss function is
given to you, and

00:32:56.081 --> 00:32:57.080
then they try to optimize it.

00:32:57.081 --> 00:33:00.780
But I actually think your
question is really insightful,

00:33:00.781 --> 00:33:02.820
because in most of those cases,

00:33:02.821 --> 00:33:05.170
that's the weak
point in the model.

00:33:05.171 --> 00:33:06.610
That thing was an assumption.

00:33:06.611 --> 00:33:08.192
Everything is an assumption.

00:33:08.193 --> 00:33:11.190
The only thing that's not
an assumption is the data.

00:33:11.191 --> 00:33:12.470
Everything else
is an assumption.

00:33:12.471 --> 00:33:14.637
And so
from the Bayesian point of view,

00:33:14.638 --> 00:33:18.248
what we really should be doing
is questioning every assumption,

00:33:18.249 --> 00:33:20.876
and not just questioning
the assumptions, but

00:33:20.877 --> 00:33:23.060
actually doing
something about that.

00:33:23.061 --> 00:33:26.380
Trying to improve on
those assumptions.

00:33:26.381 --> 00:33:27.778
So loss functions and

00:33:27.779 --> 00:33:30.190
likelihoods are also
assumptions.

00:33:30.191 --> 00:33:34.280
Let me move on, so why should we
care about this whole framework?

00:33:34.281 --> 00:33:36.596
I mean,
deep learning has been so

00:33:36.597 --> 00:33:40.571
spectacularly successful
that you would think, well,

00:33:40.572 --> 00:33:44.131
we can forget about all
this probability theory and

00:33:44.132 --> 00:33:47.689
just run whatever Adam on
my tensor flow model, and

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:49.800
I'll be fine, or whatever.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:54.428
Well, here's at least one
of the reasons I care, or

00:33:54.429 --> 00:33:56.060
two of the reasons I care,
actually.

00:33:56.061 --> 00:33:59.981
So one of them is we
really want, I think,

00:33:59.982 --> 00:34:05.361
we really want calibrated
models, where the prediction

00:34:05.362 --> 00:34:09.995
uncertainties are something
we can believe in.

00:34:09.996 --> 00:34:12.807
And it's not just
kind of a nice thing.

00:34:12.808 --> 00:34:15.130
I think it's actually
a necessary thing.

00:34:15.131 --> 00:34:19.256
We really need machine learning
systems that know when they

00:34:19.257 --> 00:34:20.250
don't know.

00:34:20.251 --> 00:34:21.943
That if they're
given a new input,

00:34:21.944 --> 00:34:25.230
they'll tell you,
I don't know what the answer is.

00:34:25.231 --> 00:34:27.578
We don't want them to be
confidently making stupid

00:34:27.579 --> 00:34:28.130
mistakes.

00:34:28.131 --> 00:34:30.700
If they're confidently
making stupid mistakes,

00:34:30.701 --> 00:34:32.390
they'll be fine in the lab.

00:34:32.391 --> 00:34:36.287
They'll never get deployed
in the real world.

00:34:36.288 --> 00:34:40.796
The other thing is that this
framework actually gives

00:34:40.797 --> 00:34:45.504
us a really nice way of doing
automatic model control and

00:34:45.505 --> 00:34:47.420
structure learning.

00:34:47.421 --> 00:34:51.226
So learning the structure of
models automatically from data.

00:34:51.227 --> 00:34:54.526
And that's really beautiful,
because of the things that

00:34:54.527 --> 00:34:58.028
annoys me the most about current
machine learning practice is

00:34:58.029 --> 00:35:00.945
that people just come up
with stuff out of thin air.

00:35:00.946 --> 00:35:05.660
Right, and it's just like
a cottage industry of people

00:35:05.661 --> 00:35:10.174
randomly inventing stuff,
testing it out a couple of

00:35:10.175 --> 00:35:16.260
times, and then saying, this
works better than other things.

00:35:16.261 --> 00:35:20.180
It's just not a systematic
science, it's a hodgepodge.

00:35:20.181 --> 00:35:22.150
It's really disappointing.

00:35:22.151 --> 00:35:24.560
And we would like to make
it a systematic science.

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:28.203
And actually, the way to make
it a systematic science is get

00:35:28.204 --> 00:35:29.324
the human out of the loop.

00:35:29.325 --> 00:35:30.767
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean, maybe,

00:35:30.768 --> 00:35:35.551
make the human do other things
or kind of oversee things.

00:35:35.552 --> 00:35:40.250
But the search over models
should be something that's

00:35:40.251 --> 00:35:43.450
done by a computer
systematically.

00:35:43.451 --> 00:35:47.367
And this framework gives us
a nice way of doing that.

00:35:47.368 --> 00:35:51.517
So just to be concrete about
what does it mean to be

00:35:51.518 --> 00:35:56.610
Bayesian, here it is in
the context of a neural network.

00:35:56.611 --> 00:35:59.240
So in the context of
a neural network,

00:35:59.241 --> 00:36:02.340
what are the sources
of uncertainty?

00:36:02.341 --> 00:36:05.311
Well, the parameters
are uncertain, right?

00:36:05.312 --> 00:36:08.220
Cuz you don't know what
the parameters are.

00:36:08.221 --> 00:36:10.010
And actually,
the structure is uncertain.

00:36:10.011 --> 00:36:13.731
People haven't really dealt
with structure uncertainty.

00:36:13.732 --> 00:36:17.210
But in an ideal world, I would
love to see both parameter and

00:36:17.211 --> 00:36:20.630
structure uncertainty dealt
with in a neural network.

00:36:20.631 --> 00:36:23.240
It just might not
be very practical.

00:36:23.241 --> 00:36:24.969
But that's why I said
in an ideal world.

00:36:27.340 --> 00:36:31.372
And actually, their sorta
world of neural networks and

00:36:31.373 --> 00:36:35.662
deep learning, and the world
of Bayesian machine learning

00:36:35.663 --> 00:36:37.930
are not in any sorta conflict.

00:36:37.931 --> 00:36:39.330
They are actually
quite compatible.

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:42.483
The two kinda live
in different spaces.

00:36:42.484 --> 00:36:45.798
So neural networks
are a kind of model,

00:36:45.799 --> 00:36:49.017
some kind of function
approximator.

00:36:49.018 --> 00:36:51.581
And Bayesian methods
are just a framework for

00:36:51.582 --> 00:36:53.800
thinking about learning.

00:36:53.801 --> 00:36:57.800
So people, for a couple of
decades at least, have thought

00:36:57.801 --> 00:37:02.894
about applying Bayesian
methods to neural networks.

00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:07.935
And so here are some examples
going back to the early 90s.

00:37:07.936 --> 00:37:10.926
People like David MacKay,
Radford Neal,

00:37:10.927 --> 00:37:13.280
Geoff Hinton worked on this.

00:37:13.281 --> 00:37:15.790
Even Yann LeCun worked on this,
even earlier,

00:37:15.791 --> 00:37:19.200
even though he didn't
realize he was doing this.

00:37:19.201 --> 00:37:20.397
He will admit to that.

00:37:20.398 --> 00:37:23.651
He was sort of analyzed from
a physics-y perspective, but

00:37:23.652 --> 00:37:26.839
it turned out, he was doing
Bayes' rule on neural network

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:28.896
rates in the late 80s.

00:37:28.897 --> 00:37:32.403
Who's sitting here has
really nice paper on

00:37:32.404 --> 00:37:35.825
Stochastic Gradient
Langevin Dynamics.

00:37:35.826 --> 00:37:39.883
It's a real beautiful idea where
you can take stochastic gradient

00:37:39.884 --> 00:37:43.142
descent, SGD, add noise to
it in a particular way and

00:37:43.143 --> 00:37:46.839
show that it approximates
actually doing Bayesian Learning

00:37:46.840 --> 00:37:48.813
in neural network parameters.

00:37:48.814 --> 00:37:52.751
And some of our work in
the machine learning group in

00:37:52.752 --> 00:37:57.057
Cambridge has also been
kindadeveloping better ways of

00:37:57.058 --> 00:38:01.740
doing Bayesian neural networks,
Bayesian deep learning.

00:38:01.741 --> 00:38:06.070
We had a workshop at NIPS,
which was great fun last year.

00:38:06.071 --> 00:38:08.955
We are trying to have
another one this year.

00:38:08.956 --> 00:38:12.940
And here is an example
of why we might care.

00:38:12.941 --> 00:38:14.090
If you have some data and

00:38:14.091 --> 00:38:17.100
you're fitting a neural network
to some input-output data,

00:38:17.101 --> 00:38:19.920
where you have data,
you can afford to be confident.

00:38:19.921 --> 00:38:23.130
But where you don't have data,
what you really want is

00:38:23.131 --> 00:38:26.070
something that shows a great
deal of uncertainty.

00:38:26.071 --> 00:38:28.500
So that's sort of the ideal
thing that we want.

00:38:29.890 --> 00:38:31.620
So when do we need
probabilities?

00:38:31.621 --> 00:38:36.591
Just to kind of summarize some
of the threads of thinking I've

00:38:36.592 --> 00:38:37.651
described.

00:38:37.652 --> 00:38:40.547
I think we need probabilities
when we want to really carefully

00:38:40.548 --> 00:38:41.805
represent uncertainty.

00:38:41.806 --> 00:38:47.051
So this includes things like
forecasting, decision making.

00:38:47.052 --> 00:38:51.728
Usually, decisions are made on
the basis of future consequences

00:38:51.729 --> 00:38:52.970
of actions.

00:38:52.971 --> 00:38:54.180
So we need to have

00:38:54.181 --> 00:38:57.880
a representation of
the consequences of our actions

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:01.900
when we're learning from
limited, noisy, or missing data.

00:39:01.901 --> 00:39:05.822
For example, in medical domains,
this is very, very useful.

00:39:05.823 --> 00:39:08.968
Or if we're doing complex
personalized modelling like in

00:39:08.969 --> 00:39:11.570
advertising, or
again, in medicine.

00:39:11.571 --> 00:39:16.330
All data compression methods are
basically probabilistic models,

00:39:16.331 --> 00:39:18.250
either implicitly or explicitly.

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:22.070
And for automating this sort
of scientific approach to

00:39:22.071 --> 00:39:25.450
modelling, this is quite useful.

00:39:25.451 --> 00:39:27.927
So now, as mentioned,

00:39:27.928 --> 00:39:32.480
I'm sort of leading Uber's AI
research right now.

00:39:32.481 --> 00:39:35.810
And so why would a company like
Uber care about something like

00:39:35.811 --> 00:39:37.160
probabilistic modeling?

00:39:37.161 --> 00:39:39.920
Well, it turns out
that actually,

00:39:39.921 --> 00:39:41.460
it's everywhere that we look.

00:39:41.461 --> 00:39:46.950
So in the research lab that
I'm trying to build at Uber,

00:39:46.951 --> 00:39:49.250
we have people with deep
learning expertise.

00:39:49.251 --> 00:39:51.190
We have people with
reinforcement learning and

00:39:51.191 --> 00:39:53.185
probabilistic modeling
expertise, and so on.

00:39:53.186 --> 00:39:56.339
They're a bunch of different
communities in there.

00:39:56.340 --> 00:40:00.049
But when we look around,
there are actually a huge number

00:40:00.050 --> 00:40:03.390
of very Clear probabilistic
modelling problems.

00:40:03.391 --> 00:40:07.744
So if you look at the kinds of
data, the kinds of modelling

00:40:07.745 --> 00:40:12.190
that we have to do to optimize
the service that we provide,

00:40:12.191 --> 00:40:17.005
this involves understanding
the spatial temporal dynamics in

00:40:17.006 --> 00:40:21.816
being able to forecast that for
both drivers which is the supply

00:40:21.817 --> 00:40:25.420
and riders which is
the demand side.

00:40:25.421 --> 00:40:30.140
We have to be able to model ETA,
how long it takes for

00:40:30.141 --> 00:40:34.990
food or person or
package to get from A to B,

00:40:34.991 --> 00:40:41.510
we use models in all of our
forecasting and planning.

00:40:41.511 --> 00:40:43.916
We're modeling cities,
we're modeling traffic.

00:40:43.917 --> 00:40:45.880
It's all about modeling stuff.

00:40:45.881 --> 00:40:49.930
And these are complicated
systems to model.

00:40:49.931 --> 00:40:53.248
And so we can use combinations
of deep learning and

00:40:53.249 --> 00:40:57.593
sort of function approximators,
and probabilistic modeling to

00:40:57.594 --> 00:41:00.595
build sophisticated
forecasting models and

00:41:00.596 --> 00:41:03.740
then to optimize
the system on top of that.

00:41:03.741 --> 00:41:06.230
So the service is just
better for everybody.

00:41:06.231 --> 00:41:09.609
And that is, I said all of that
and I'm not even talking about

00:41:09.610 --> 00:41:12.925
self-driving cars which is
a whole, now there are several

00:41:12.926 --> 00:41:16.060
hundred people working on
self-driving cars at Uber.

00:41:16.061 --> 00:41:20.108
And there are many modeling

00:41:20.109 --> 00:41:25.039
problems as well in that domain.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:29.760
And of course, we're also try to
build personal aviation service

00:41:29.761 --> 00:41:33.650
which I'm sure will come
with this modeling problem.

00:41:33.651 --> 00:41:38.750
This is by 2020 we have
committed to doing trials in

00:41:38.751 --> 00:41:43.510
Dubai and Dallas of what you and
I would all call flying cars.

00:41:43.511 --> 00:41:46.880
But the guys who run that at
Uber hate the term flying cars.

00:41:46.881 --> 00:41:50.432
Cuz they don't actually drive on
the road, they're sort of like

00:41:50.433 --> 00:41:53.290
giant drones that you get
into and you fly from A to B.

00:41:53.291 --> 00:41:55.882
So watch out In
the next few years, but

00:41:55.883 --> 00:41:59.510
this is part of what
we're also trying to do.

00:41:59.511 --> 00:42:04.860
And again, although initially
the vertical takeoff and

00:42:04.861 --> 00:42:08.800
landing vehicles
will be piloted,

00:42:08.801 --> 00:42:12.570
eventually these will be
autonomous electrical vehicles.

00:42:12.571 --> 00:42:17.430
So all the same sorts of
inference perception and

00:42:17.431 --> 00:42:22.060
modeling problems that
occur in the self driving

00:42:22.061 --> 00:42:24.130
domain will also occur
in the flying domain.

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:32.103
So that's it for the uber stuff.

00:42:32.104 --> 00:42:36.781
For the rest of the time that
I have, a few more minutes,

00:42:36.782 --> 00:42:41.160
what I wanna do is I wanna
talk about three areas that I

00:42:41.161 --> 00:42:44.945
think are really exciting
at the cutting edge

00:42:44.946 --> 00:42:48.150
of machine learning and
AI right now.

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:53.580
And these are areas
that my group and

00:42:53.581 --> 00:42:56.090
myself are working on,
that we're very excited about.

00:42:56.091 --> 00:42:58.501
I've already talked a little bit
about Bayesian deep learning.

00:42:58.502 --> 00:43:01.360
I'm not gonna talk
much more about that.

00:43:01.361 --> 00:43:06.520
I'm basically gonna talk about
probabilistic programming,

00:43:06.521 --> 00:43:08.010
the automatic statistician, and

00:43:08.011 --> 00:43:10.400
Bayesian optimization,
very briefly on each of those.

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:14.450
So let's start with
probabilistic programming.

00:43:15.540 --> 00:43:18.176
The idea of probabilistic
programming is all

00:43:18.177 --> 00:43:21.019
this probabilistic modeling
stuff is nice, but

00:43:21.020 --> 00:43:24.765
it's really time consuming to
define a probabilistic model and

00:43:24.766 --> 00:43:27.054
then derive an inference
algorithm and

00:43:27.055 --> 00:43:29.781
implement the inference
algorithm and so on.

00:43:29.782 --> 00:43:32.059
And it's not just
time consuming, but

00:43:32.060 --> 00:43:33.930
it's incredibly error prone.

00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:38.530
The solution to that
comes in the form

00:43:38.531 --> 00:43:41.090
of this field of
probabilistic programming.

00:43:41.091 --> 00:43:43.670
And the idea is a two-part idea.

00:43:43.671 --> 00:43:44.960
One of them is,

00:43:44.961 --> 00:43:48.600
instead of defining your model
as a bunch of equations, or

00:43:48.601 --> 00:43:51.320
something like that or
a graphical model, a diagram

00:43:51.321 --> 00:43:56.020
in a paper, define your model
as a computer program, okay.

00:43:56.021 --> 00:43:58.639
The computer program
that can generate data.

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:02.076
Now it could be a conditional
data generator,

00:44:02.077 --> 00:44:06.550
like if you're trying to
generate y as a function of x.

00:44:06.551 --> 00:44:08.850
The program could take an x and

00:44:08.851 --> 00:44:13.480
produce possible ys,
that's a conditional program.

00:44:13.481 --> 00:44:15.877
Or it could be a generative
model of like x,

00:44:15.878 --> 00:44:19.543
like if you're trying to build a
generative model for language or

00:44:19.544 --> 00:44:23.143
speech or something like that it
could generate the whole signal

00:44:23.144 --> 00:44:23.693
for you.

00:44:23.694 --> 00:44:25.980
In any case,
it's a computer program.

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:30.200
And that idea is obviously very
old, like we have simulators.

00:44:30.201 --> 00:44:33.450
Many, many fields of science and
engineering have relied on

00:44:33.451 --> 00:44:35.810
simulators, which are computer
programs for their models.

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:40.290
The really interesting bit
of probabilistic programming

00:44:40.291 --> 00:44:44.930
is this second part,
which is given your model,

00:44:44.931 --> 00:44:48.180
defined as a simulator,
we can actually define

00:44:48.181 --> 00:44:51.500
a Universal Inference Engine,
and that is

00:44:51.501 --> 00:44:56.430
a system that will basically do
Bayes rule on computer programs.

00:44:56.431 --> 00:44:58.407
So what does that mean
actually in practice?

00:44:58.408 --> 00:45:00.872
What that means is I
have a simulator for

00:45:00.873 --> 00:45:04.620
potential data which has a bunch
of random variables in it.

00:45:04.621 --> 00:45:06.406
But now, I have some real data,

00:45:06.407 --> 00:45:09.430
that's the data I've
actually observed.

00:45:09.431 --> 00:45:11.960
And what I'd like to do is
I would like to figure out,

00:45:13.910 --> 00:45:18.900
what are the plausible hidden
variables or random variables in

00:45:18.901 --> 00:45:22.070
my simulator that are consistent
with my real data?

00:45:23.330 --> 00:45:24.470
If I can do that,

00:45:24.471 --> 00:45:27.800
that's actually solving Bayes'
rule on my computer program.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:32.160
Well, let's think
about it this way.

00:45:32.161 --> 00:45:34.060
We're all used to running
computer programs in

00:45:34.061 --> 00:45:35.000
the forward direction.

00:45:35.001 --> 00:45:37.470
You give them inputs and
they produce outputs.

00:45:37.471 --> 00:45:38.650
This is like

00:45:38.651 --> 00:45:41.390
thinking about running them
in the backward direction.

00:45:41.391 --> 00:45:44.720
Given that I know what the
outputs are, that was the data.

00:45:44.721 --> 00:45:49.430
What are the calls for
the random number generator

00:45:49.431 --> 00:45:51.240
that are consistent
with those outputs?

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:53.380
That's the basic concept.

00:45:54.410 --> 00:45:57.900
And this sounds kind
of like magic, but

00:45:57.901 --> 00:45:59.930
it's actually possible.

00:45:59.931 --> 00:46:03.280
And there'd been a whole
bunch of systems here

00:46:03.281 --> 00:46:05.420
around that have
developed on this idea.

00:46:05.421 --> 00:46:08.731
And I wanna call out,
in particular, a system that

00:46:08.732 --> 00:46:12.350
was developed at Microsoft
research, in particular,

00:46:12.351 --> 00:46:16.058
in this lab here, which was
really way ahead of its time.

00:46:16.059 --> 00:46:21.451
Over ten years ago, infer.NET
was really like the most

00:46:21.452 --> 00:46:27.660
powerful policy programming
language for scalable modeling.

00:46:29.630 --> 00:46:30.214
But by now,

00:46:30.215 --> 00:46:32.848
we have a whole bunch of
different languages that have

00:46:32.849 --> 00:46:35.483
been developed including
sharing which is a language

00:46:35.484 --> 00:46:38.600
developed in my group and I will
show you some examples of that.

00:46:39.960 --> 00:46:43.070
And then under the hood,
what's happening is

00:46:43.071 --> 00:46:45.450
that we have a whole bunch
of inference algorithms.

00:46:45.451 --> 00:46:48.110
These are methods
that can be used to

00:46:48.111 --> 00:46:52.040
figure out what those hidden
variables are, giving the data.

00:46:52.041 --> 00:46:56.227
And these are various forms
of Markov chain Monte Carlo,

00:46:56.228 --> 00:47:00.513
variation inference, or
particle filter and etcetera.

00:47:03.474 --> 00:47:09.010
Here's an example from
the programming language Turing.

00:47:10.760 --> 00:47:13.210
Maybe what I'll do is
I'll play you the video.

00:47:15.190 --> 00:47:18.984
It's just easier to
explain I think.

00:47:26.829 --> 00:47:33.037
Okay, so let me just make sure.

00:47:41.646 --> 00:47:43.370
Okay, I guess that's right.

00:47:43.371 --> 00:47:45.941
All right, so
if you're familiar with,

00:47:45.942 --> 00:47:49.103
how many people know what
a hidden Markov model is?

00:47:49.104 --> 00:47:51.050
Raise your hand if you know it.

00:47:51.051 --> 00:47:52.410
Okay, good, almost everybody.

00:47:54.560 --> 00:47:57.330
So here is a hidden
Markov model defined

00:47:57.331 --> 00:48:01.900
in this probabilistic
programming language, Turing.

00:48:01.901 --> 00:48:06.253
The main things that are here
are this model construct.

00:48:06.254 --> 00:48:10.851
Turing is based on Julia, a kind
of nice, modern scientific

00:48:10.852 --> 00:48:14.638
programming language which
is meant to be a kind of

00:48:14.639 --> 00:48:18.710
a replacement for MathLab and
r and everything else.

00:48:21.220 --> 00:48:25.910
And here's an h and m defined,
and the main thing to notice is

00:48:25.911 --> 00:48:28.848
this little tilde character
which is drawn from.

00:48:28.849 --> 00:48:32.270
So this define as it's
drawn from the categorical

00:48:32.271 --> 00:48:35.150
distribution, either the states
have it in Markov Model, and

00:48:35.151 --> 00:48:36.050
these are the outputs,

00:48:36.051 --> 00:48:38.640
like a normal, drawn from
a normal distribution.

00:48:38.641 --> 00:48:41.640
This, I would argue,
is about as natural and

00:48:41.641 --> 00:48:44.540
compact representation
of an HMM,

00:48:44.541 --> 00:48:46.910
as I would imagine having
in a programming language.

00:48:46.911 --> 00:48:49.291
So, the surface form
is really nice.

00:48:49.292 --> 00:48:53.377
In, for instance like this, you
say I'm gonna do sampling on my

00:48:53.378 --> 00:48:57.323
HMM, and I'm gonna use particle
GIBs with some parameters.

00:48:57.324 --> 00:49:00.669
So this is a call to
the inference algorithm,

00:49:00.670 --> 00:49:03.322
you don't have to
implement that.

00:49:05.394 --> 00:49:11.145
And then what happens
is you run this.

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:19.870
And a few minutes later,
it actually has done inference.

00:49:19.871 --> 00:49:23.172
This is just a visualization of
the hidden states over time,

00:49:23.173 --> 00:49:26.550
which is a bit boring, they're
jumping around a little bit.

00:49:26.551 --> 00:49:31.746
I'll just show another example
maybe, and then we can move on.

00:49:31.747 --> 00:49:37.133
Okay, so here is another example
of a Hidden Markov Model,

00:49:37.134 --> 00:49:42.535
but what this is, is it's
a Bayesian Hidden Markov Model.

00:49:42.536 --> 00:49:45.306
Bayesian Hidden Markov Model,
by this we mean,

00:49:45.307 --> 00:49:48.620
a Hidden Markov Model where
we're actually representing

00:49:48.621 --> 00:49:51.190
the uncertainty in
the transition matrix and

00:49:51.191 --> 00:49:53.565
in the parameters of
the emission model.

00:49:53.566 --> 00:49:58.331
So we just add a couple
four extra lines to this,

00:49:58.332 --> 00:50:03.000
to turn our previous code
into a Bayes and HMM.

00:50:03.001 --> 00:50:07.172
And again we can do it and
solve that In this case, it's

00:50:07.173 --> 00:50:11.983
a combination of Hamiltonian
Monte Carlo and particle gibbs,

00:50:11.984 --> 00:50:17.099
all wrapped into an outer loop
of inference, with gibbs.

00:50:17.100 --> 00:50:23.040
And this is just a visualization
of inference on that.

00:50:23.041 --> 00:50:26.304
And now, it has some of
uncertainty about where

00:50:26.305 --> 00:50:29.733
the means of the different
observations are, and

00:50:29.734 --> 00:50:32.068
that's shown here.

00:50:32.069 --> 00:50:40.230
Okay, and
maybe I'll show one last one.

00:50:40.231 --> 00:50:43.740
So this is just defining
a mixture of categoricals.

00:50:43.741 --> 00:50:44.730
Let's jump ahead.

00:50:49.829 --> 00:50:53.290
This is defining
Latent Dirichlet allocation.

00:50:53.291 --> 00:50:56.778
Again, the code for Latent
Dirichlet allocation is really

00:50:56.779 --> 00:50:58.810
pretty straightforward, okay.

00:50:58.811 --> 00:51:02.255
So it's sort of nice and
abstract that way, okay.

00:51:13.075 --> 00:51:15.932
So that was probabilistic
programming.

00:51:15.933 --> 00:51:17.428
Let me move on, question?
Yeah.

00:51:17.429 --> 00:51:19.018
&gt;&gt; Is there any restriction
on what effects you can do?

00:51:19.019 --> 00:51:20.954
Or are they all just MCMC,

00:51:20.955 --> 00:51:21.838
kind of-
&gt;&gt; So

00:51:21.839 --> 00:51:26.494
the restriction is on stuff
that we've implemented, okay.

00:51:28.010 --> 00:51:30.942
And so, there are a few
algorithms we've implemented

00:51:30.943 --> 00:51:31.840
under the hood.

00:51:31.841 --> 00:51:36.334
My team is implementing
some other ones.

00:51:36.335 --> 00:51:38.190
Different probabilistic
programming languages have

00:51:38.191 --> 00:51:39.890
different things that
people have implemented.

00:51:39.891 --> 00:51:44.840
So we've done various versions
of particle filtering,

00:51:44.841 --> 00:51:49.090
MCMC with
Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, NUTS.

00:51:49.091 --> 00:51:51.710
We're trying to reproduce
what Stan does, and

00:51:51.711 --> 00:51:53.160
benchmark against that.

00:51:53.161 --> 00:51:56.403
I mean, completely honestly,
it's a very nice framework cuz

00:51:56.404 --> 00:51:58.950
it's all naturally
embedded in Julia.

00:51:58.951 --> 00:52:02.640
It's not as fast as a language
like Stan because that's

00:52:02.641 --> 00:52:06.870
actually optimized for finite
models whereas we can actually

00:52:06.871 --> 00:52:09.490
deal with a broader
class of models.

00:52:09.491 --> 00:52:12.552
And we're working on making it
all much more efficient and

00:52:12.553 --> 00:52:13.186
scalable.

00:52:13.187 --> 00:52:16.104
And this is what's going
on actually, interestingly,

00:52:16.105 --> 00:52:19.070
I have an effort like this
going on at Cambridge.

00:52:19.071 --> 00:52:23.950
And the Uber AI labs know
of good men who's one of

00:52:23.951 --> 00:52:28.220
the pioneers of probalistic
programming is working at Uber

00:52:28.221 --> 00:52:32.150
now and we're developing
a scalable kind of industry

00:52:32.151 --> 00:52:35.010
strength probalistic programming
language at Uber as well.

00:52:35.011 --> 00:52:38.344
&gt;&gt; Can you define
how scalable it is?

00:52:38.345 --> 00:52:43.282
These frameworks not
only take time, but

00:52:43.283 --> 00:52:46.230
are not very scalable.

00:52:46.231 --> 00:52:47.187
&gt;&gt; In terms of data?
&gt;&gt; And in computation.

00:52:47.188 --> 00:52:50.989
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, the target that
we have is as scalable as deep

00:52:50.990 --> 00:52:52.545
learning frameworks.

00:52:52.546 --> 00:52:53.779
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:52:53.780 --> 00:52:56.664
&gt;&gt; Okay, so parallelizable,
run on GPUs, etc.

00:52:56.665 --> 00:52:59.907
And in fact, we're
piggybacking on top of deep

00:52:59.908 --> 00:53:02.989
learning kind of
frameworks that exist, and

00:53:02.990 --> 00:53:07.223
adding a layer of probabilistic
programming to them, yeah?

00:53:07.224 --> 00:53:09.393
&gt;&gt; Related to
scalability advantage,

00:53:09.394 --> 00:53:12.683
I don't know this question
is in the right context, but

00:53:12.684 --> 00:53:16.393
what happens if you're learning
parameters that are actually

00:53:16.394 --> 00:53:20.370
related to each other,
being conflict with each other?

00:53:20.371 --> 00:53:23.233
Does that then make it that
you need to learn covariance

00:53:23.234 --> 00:53:25.956
matrices, as well, or-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, in theory,

00:53:25.957 --> 00:53:30.249
if you have a model with many
parameters, in the posterior,

00:53:30.250 --> 00:53:32.860
the parameters will
be correlated,

00:53:32.861 --> 00:53:36.520
even if in the prior,
they're not correlated.

00:53:36.521 --> 00:53:40.649
In practice, what people do
is either they ignore that,

00:53:40.650 --> 00:53:44.780
which is an approximation, or
they use a sampling method

00:53:44.781 --> 00:53:48.754
where you can infer the
correlation from the samples.

00:53:48.755 --> 00:53:50.443
Let me move on cuz, I mean,

00:53:50.444 --> 00:53:53.540
I've been taking some
questions along the way.

00:53:53.541 --> 00:53:55.763
But I wanna leave at least
a couple of minutes for

00:53:55.764 --> 00:53:57.160
questions at the end as well.

00:53:57.161 --> 00:54:00.867
I'm gonna move on to very
quickly talk about the two other

00:54:00.868 --> 00:54:03.117
ideas, Bayesian Optimization.

00:54:03.118 --> 00:54:06.735
The idea of Bayesian
Optimization is that we wanna do

00:54:06.736 --> 00:54:10.603
global optimization of some
black-box functions that

00:54:10.604 --> 00:54:12.800
are expensive to evaluate.

00:54:12.801 --> 00:54:14.550
So this is the basic idea,

00:54:14.551 --> 00:54:17.990
find the arg max of f(x)
as a function of x.

00:54:17.991 --> 00:54:21.110
Very simple idea but
the key thing is,

00:54:21.111 --> 00:54:25.690
what if evaluating f(x)
costs quite a lot of time or

00:54:25.691 --> 00:54:28.439
money or energy or
whatever it is?

00:54:29.890 --> 00:54:31.070
What do you do then?

00:54:31.071 --> 00:54:36.280
Well, the right thing to do then
is you treat this as a problem

00:54:36.281 --> 00:54:39.460
in sequential
decision-making and

00:54:39.461 --> 00:54:42.740
you model the uncertainty that
you have in the function f

00:54:42.741 --> 00:54:45.160
to figure out what
the optimal actions are.

00:54:45.161 --> 00:54:47.620
And this has just a huge
number of applications.

00:54:47.621 --> 00:54:51.353
And it's important to think
beyond the little world of

00:54:51.354 --> 00:54:55.328
machine learning to think about
all of the other aspects of

00:54:55.329 --> 00:54:56.139
science and

00:54:56.140 --> 00:55:01.010
engineering that might
involve optimizing something.

00:55:01.011 --> 00:55:02.490
Anywhere we can
optimize something,

00:55:02.491 --> 00:55:04.626
we can actually do a Bayesian
Optimization, I think.

00:55:04.627 --> 00:55:10.140
If certain criteria hold.

00:55:10.141 --> 00:55:13.000
This is just Bayesian
optimization in a nutshell.

00:55:13.001 --> 00:55:14.990
The thing that drives it is,

00:55:14.991 --> 00:55:17.600
you're making decisions about
where to evaluate the function.

00:55:17.601 --> 00:55:18.770
This is the function,

00:55:18.771 --> 00:55:21.340
let's say, you're uncertain
about the function,

00:55:21.341 --> 00:55:25.420
that's why it's sort of shown
with the shaded region.

00:55:25.421 --> 00:55:26.854
You've evaluated some data.

00:55:26.855 --> 00:55:28.414
This is why you're
being Bayesian.

00:55:28.415 --> 00:55:30.620
You're actually trying to
represent the uncertainty that

00:55:30.621 --> 00:55:32.150
you have in your function.

00:55:32.151 --> 00:55:35.803
And then, based on that
uncertainty, what you'll have is

00:55:35.804 --> 00:55:39.459
something called an acquisition
function which is telling

00:55:39.460 --> 00:55:42.762
you where it's best to
evaluate your function next.

00:55:42.763 --> 00:55:44.818
It's sort of trading
off exploration and

00:55:44.819 --> 00:55:47.410
exploitation cuz it's
a decision making problem.

00:55:49.180 --> 00:55:50.560
So where is this useful?

00:55:50.561 --> 00:55:53.769
I've already said I actually
think it's useful to think about

00:55:53.770 --> 00:55:55.227
any optimization problem.

00:55:55.228 --> 00:55:58.509
In particular, if evaluating
the function is expensive,

00:55:58.510 --> 00:56:01.340
if evaluating derivatives
is hard or impossible.

00:56:02.350 --> 00:56:05.000
If there is noise in
the function evaluations, or

00:56:05.001 --> 00:56:07.910
there may be noisy constraints.

00:56:07.911 --> 00:56:10.610
Or there is prior information
about the function that you

00:56:10.611 --> 00:56:12.940
might wanna use in
optimization or

00:56:12.941 --> 00:56:15.220
if you need to optimize
many similar functions.

00:56:15.221 --> 00:56:18.003
So in all of these contexts
actually helps to think about

00:56:18.004 --> 00:56:19.740
things in terms of
Bayesian optimisation.

00:56:21.380 --> 00:56:25.090
So the last thing I wanna talk
about is very, very quickly,

00:56:25.091 --> 00:56:27.776
this idea of
the Automatic Statistician.

00:56:27.777 --> 00:56:31.302
And the idea here is that
we have a lot of data, but

00:56:31.303 --> 00:56:35.160
we don't have enough
data scientists.

00:56:35.161 --> 00:56:36.720
So what can we do?

00:56:36.721 --> 00:56:39.090
We can super power
the data scientists or

00:56:39.091 --> 00:56:41.160
we can replace
the data scientists.

00:56:41.161 --> 00:56:45.050
And that's really the idea of
building systems that automate

00:56:45.051 --> 00:56:46.356
model discovery from data.

00:56:46.357 --> 00:56:49.632
And I see Riota standing up.

00:56:49.633 --> 00:56:54.534
That's what happens when you
take questions in an already too

00:56:54.535 --> 00:56:55.579
long a talk.

00:56:55.580 --> 00:56:57.230
So no, no, no, it's fine.

00:56:57.231 --> 00:56:59.822
We can sort of run through it.

00:56:59.823 --> 00:57:02.651
Maybe I'll just show you the
video which only takes a couple

00:57:02.652 --> 00:57:03.280
of minutes.

00:57:04.970 --> 00:57:09.869
So this system basically takes
raw data in the form of a table.

00:57:09.870 --> 00:57:12.154
It searches over good models.

00:57:12.155 --> 00:57:13.258
It finds a good model.

00:57:13.259 --> 00:57:18.105
And then it turns that
model into a report that

00:57:18.106 --> 00:57:23.840
describes patterns in
the data to a human user.

00:57:23.841 --> 00:57:27.830
And the key thing is that

00:57:27.831 --> 00:57:31.210
this is really meant to be
the opposite of a black box.

00:57:31.211 --> 00:57:32.680
You really want
a transparent box.

00:57:32.681 --> 00:57:37.565
You want the computer to
explain in English what is

00:57:37.566 --> 00:57:40.016
found in the data to you.

00:57:40.017 --> 00:57:44.275
So here is just a demo of the
Automatic Statistician running

00:57:44.276 --> 00:57:47.264
on a classic,
tiny data set called Iris.

00:57:47.265 --> 00:57:49.277
So you load up the data.

00:57:51.388 --> 00:57:52.968
And then, it turns away and

00:57:52.969 --> 00:57:55.311
thinks about lots of
different models.

00:57:55.312 --> 00:57:58.723
And then what
happens is you wait.

00:58:09.696 --> 00:58:11.722
It says your report
is being prepared.

00:58:11.723 --> 00:58:14.340
This is the report
that it prepares.

00:58:14.341 --> 00:58:15.590
It's actually found,

00:58:15.591 --> 00:58:19.180
it's evaluated a few
different models.

00:58:19.181 --> 00:58:21.470
Actually, what happened there?

00:58:21.471 --> 00:58:23.323
What happened was that
the report was actually

00:58:23.324 --> 00:58:24.160
a dynamic object.

00:58:24.161 --> 00:58:26.208
So it keep thinking
about models and

00:58:26.209 --> 00:58:29.015
it keeps updating the report
which is a web page.

00:58:29.016 --> 00:58:31.924
Now the report is sort of
finished and this is a very

00:58:31.925 --> 00:58:35.197
basic version where it's
doing some visualizations.

00:58:35.198 --> 00:58:37.789
What it decided was
that the best model for

00:58:37.790 --> 00:58:41.124
explaining what's in the data
is to assume that there

00:58:41.125 --> 00:58:43.438
are just three
clusters in the data.

00:58:43.439 --> 00:58:46.753
Which actually, if you know
Iris, is not a bad assumption,

00:58:46.754 --> 00:58:48.705
sort of discovered
that on its own.

00:58:48.706 --> 00:58:53.322
And then it produces a whole
bunch of visualizations that

00:58:53.323 --> 00:58:58.427
show you that there's hypothesis
of three clusters in the data

00:59:03.147 --> 00:59:08.973
Okay, so, I think I'm now
basically ready to move,

00:59:08.974 --> 00:59:11.830
jump to the conclusions.

00:59:12.930 --> 00:59:14.850
That's just
the Automatic Statistician.

00:59:14.851 --> 00:59:18.487
We've done it for
classification as well.

00:59:18.488 --> 00:59:23.187
And I wanna conclude by saying a
lot of the things I've described

00:59:23.188 --> 00:59:27.102
in this talk are in this
